  "reasoning": "The patient presents with nausea, persistent chest pain, shivering, and significant anxiety. This combination of gastrointestinal distress (nausea, chest/epigastric pain) and psychological symptoms (anxiety) strongly aligns with peptic ulcer disease. Stress and anxiety are well-known triggers and companions of peptic ulcers, frequently exacerbating pain and nausea. While shivering may suggest chills, in the absence of high fever, productive cough, or rash, it is likely a physiological response to pain or autonomic arousal from anxiety. Other conditions are less consistent: hypertension typically features headaches and dizziness without nausea; gastroesophageal reflux disease emphasizes heartburn, belching, and sour taste; and infectious diseases like malaria or typhoid would prominently display high fever and distinct systemic signs. Therefore, the clinical picture best matches peptic ulcer disease.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]peptic ulcer disease[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
